# The auxiliary ULPI transceiver of a Great Scott Gadgets Cynthion r1.4,
# wired as a USB device: the same eight bidirectional data balls
# `bidir_bus.rcf` turns around, now used for what they are for.
#
# Every line is Great Scott Gadgets' own, from
# `cynthion/python/src/gateware/platform/cynthion_r1_4.py`:
#
#     ULPIResource("aux_phy", 0,
#         data="F16 G15 G16 H15 J15 J16 K15 K16", clk="D16", clk_dir='o',
#         dir="E16", nxt="F15", stp="E15", rst="J13", rst_invert=True,
#         attrs=Attrs(IO_TYPE="LVCMOS33", SLEWRATE="FAST")),
#
# and the platform file's own `default_usb_connection = "aux_phy"`, which is
# the port their gateware puts a USB device on. `apollo_port_sharing` names
# only `control_phy`, so the AUX port is nobody else's: loading this cannot
# take away the Apollo debugger this board is programmed over.
#
# What each line is, and which of them a wrong direction would damage:
#
#   data   eight balls, bidirectional, driven by the FPGA only while the
#          transceiver is not driving them. `usb_ulpi_device.v`'s header and
#          `bidir_bus.v`'s have the argument; the interlock is `ulpi_dir`.
#   dir    an input. The transceiver drives it and the FPGA must not.
#   nxt    an input, the transceiver's throttle.
#   stp    an output, one cycle at the end of a transfer.
#   rst    an output, **active low at the ball** (`rst_invert=True`), which
#          is what the transceiver's own reset pin is. The core's
#          `ulpi_rst_n` is already in that sense, so nothing inverts here.
#   clk    an output: `clk_dir='o'` means the FPGA drives the interface
#          clock. 60 MHz both ways, so A8's own net reaches D16 and no PLL
#          is involved.
#
# `iodb.json` puts all of data, dir, nxt, stp and rst at column 72 — the
# right edge — and the eight data balls in **three** pad tiles, three of
# which hold more than one bidirectional pad. `bidir_bus.rcf` has that
# table and `docs/fpga-trellis.md` has why it mattered.
#
# **No `-pullup` on anything.** The far end of every one of these nets is a
# transceiver pin; an internal pull would be fighting the board.
# `configure_io` writes `PULLMODE = NONE`, which is what the platform file
# asks for too — it names `IO_TYPE` and `SLEWRATE` and no `PULLMODE`.
#
# `SLEWRATE="FAST"` is asked for on every one of the thirteen ULPI pins below,
# with `-slew fast`, because the platform file asks for it on all of them —
# `attrs=` on a `ULPIResource` reaches every subsignal, inputs included, and
# nextpnr writes `PIO<s>.SLEWRATE` from the attribute and not from the
# direction. It costs exactly thirteen bits. **It changed nothing on the
# part**: "An edge rate on every ULPI pin" in `docs/fpga-trellis.md` has the
# measurement, and the device still fails the same way.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# A8, THE OSCILLATOR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     # Primary, discrete 60MHz oscillator.
#     Resource("clk_60MHz", 0, Pins("A8", dir="i"),
#         Clock(60e6), Attrs(IO_TYPE="LVCMOS33")),
#
# ULPI's interface clock is 60 MHz and so is this, which is the whole reason
# there is no `usb_device_ulpi_pll` beside the core the way there is a
# `usb_device_fs_pll`. A8 is at row 0, column 29, PIO B, bank 0 — the top
# edge, on the `PCLKC` half of bank 0's differential clock pair, so the
# clock reaches its buffer through ordinary interconnect. `clock_blink.rcf`
# has that walk.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE LEDS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     *LEDResources(pins="E13 C13 B14 A15 D12 C11",
#                   attrs=Attrs(IO_TYPE="LVCMOS33"), invert=True),
#
# Active low, LED 0 is E13, all six on the top edge in bank 1. They are the
# diagnosis for a device that does not appear; the observable is `lsusb`.
# `usb_ulpi_device.v` says what each one means and in what order to read
# them.
#
# The USER button is deliberately **not** constrained. A manual reset would
# need the core's `rst_n`, which is asynchronous, and
# `configure_registers` refuses an asynchronous reset on this family.

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 clk A8

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[0] F16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[1] G15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[2] G16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[3] H15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[4] J15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[5] J16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[6] K15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[7] K16

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_dir E16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_nxt F15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_stp E15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_rst_n J13
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_clk D16

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led0_n E13
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led1_n C13
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led2_n B14
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led3_n A15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led4_n D12
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led5_n C11
